@page
@{
    var hour = DateTime.Now.Hour;
}

<p>
    HTML and CSS make a page that looks the same for everyone. To make a page that can count,
    make choices, or change, you need a programming language. This website uses <strong>C#</strong>.
</p>
<p>
    The C# runs on the <strong>server</strong>. It builds the HTML, and then the server sends that
    HTML to the browser. The browser never sees your C# code.
</p>

<h2>What is Razor?</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Razor</strong> lets you mix C# and HTML in the same file. Razor files end in
    <code>.cshtml</code>. The most important rule is:
</p>
<p class="note"><strong>The @@ symbol switches from HTML to C#.</strong></p>

<h2>Showing a value</h2>
<pre>
@@{
    var name = "Sam";
}
&lt;p&gt;Hi, @@name!&lt;/p&gt;
</pre>
<p>
    The <code>@@{ }</code> block holds C# code. <code>@@name</code> puts the value of the variable
    into the page. The browser gets <code>&lt;p&gt;Hi, Sam!&lt;/p&gt;</code>.
</p>
<p>
    This page is doing it right now: the server's clock says <strong>@DateTime.Now.ToString("h:mm tt")</strong>.
    Refresh the page and the time changes.
</p>

<h2>Making choices with if</h2>
<pre>
@@if (hour &lt; 12)
{
    &lt;p&gt;Good morning!&lt;/p&gt;
}
else
{
    &lt;p&gt;Good afternoon!&lt;/p&gt;
}
</pre>
<p>
    Right now this would say:
    @if (hour < 12)
    {
        <strong>Good morning!</strong>
    }
    else
    {
        <strong>Good afternoon!</strong>
    }
</p>

<h2>Repeating with foreach</h2>
<pre>
@@{
    var snacks = new[] { "Pizza", "Tacos", "Popcorn" };
}
&lt;ul&gt;
    @@foreach (var snack in snacks)
    {
        &lt;li&gt;@@snack&lt;/li&gt;
    }
&lt;/ul&gt;
</pre>
<p>
    This writes one <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> for each snack. The home page uses a loop like this to
    list every lesson from <code>Models/Lesson.cs</code>.
</p>

<h2>Files and web addresses</h2>
<p>Each file in the <code>Pages</code> folder becomes a web address:</p>
<table>
    <tr><th>File</th><th>Address</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Index.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Lessons/Html.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/Lessons/Html</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Lessons/Razor.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/Lessons/Razor</code></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Layouts</h2>
<p>
    The menu and footer are not copied into every page. They are in one file,
    <code>Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml</code>. The line <code>@@RenderBody()</code> is where each
    page's content goes. The lessons also use <code>Pages/Lessons/_LessonLayout.cshtml</code>,
    which adds the lesson title and the Back and Next links.
</p>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Showing a real @@ sign:</strong> Because @@ means "C# starts here," you type it twice
    (<code>@@@@</code>) to show one on the page.
</p>
